WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:06.120
So the core hypotheses that I'm
developing in my research are that an

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:11.059
inclusive national narrative, one that's
based on creeds,

00:00:11.059 --> 00:00:15.900
ideas, principles—some of you've
read Fukuyama's recent work on

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:22.710
identity, he calls this "credo-nationalism"—that that, all else being equal, helps

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:28.050
actually to support democracy. Whereas an
exclusive national narrative—one that's

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:33.180
based on fixed identities of race,
religion, caste, ethnicity, religion being

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:39.180
fixed in most parts of the world as an
identity—undermines democracy, all else

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:44.190
being equal. And before I kind of
unpack those hypotheses and what I'm

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:50.190
doing to research and test
these, I want to I wanted to spend a

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:55.289
moment on two questions I often get
asked when I present this research. So

00:00:55.289 --> 00:00:59.489
the first question is: am I not really
collapsing the distinction between

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:04.049
patriotism and nationalism, right? So
isn't patriotism all the good

00:01:04.049 --> 00:01:08.580
stuff about feeling love for one's
country and nationalism all the bad

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:14.369
stuff? Well, no serious scholar that I
know of nationalism actually takes that

00:01:14.369 --> 00:01:19.170
distinction very seriously. It's a very
big part of the public debate, but the

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:25.110
very same actions can look, can be
described as patriotic or nationalist

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:29.369
depending on whether you like them or
not, and what outcomes you think that

00:01:29.369 --> 00:01:37.200
they have. But to describe a phenomenon analytically, it's important not to

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.950
describe it based on its consequences,
but to be able to describe the

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:48.540
phenomenon in and of itself. And love for
one's country certainly can just be part

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:55.920
of, because...So patriotism can, some
research has shown, quite easily slide

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:01.170
into nationalism,
especially when circumstances of threat

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:09.720
arise. The second question I get asked a
lot is: isn't the ideal world one

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.340
in which we don't have national
identities at all?

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:18.810
Why celebrate national identities? Why really promote them in some sense if

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:26.980
the ideal world is one in which we all
just...our primary identities are ones

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:34.060
that are based on our common humanity.
And the answer I give to that is that

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:38.890
there's a lot of social psychology
research that shows that human beings

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:46.150
are by their very nature clannish; they
need communities. And that in fact the

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:50.620
countries around the world that we've
seen that don't have a national identity,

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:55.660
that've come closest to not having a
national identity, are not utopian but in

00:02:55.660 --> 00:03:02.739
fact very dystopian. And what you see
here is a picture of Somalia, where there

00:03:02.739 --> 00:03:09.910
is no clear national identity at all.
So I go about this research, as I said

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:13.079
I'm at the very beginning of this
project, so this is a sort of a,

00:03:13.079 --> 00:03:18.670
relatively light on a lot of evidence.
But I'm I'm going to tell you about how

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:23.680
I'm going about this research in
three domains. The first domain is that

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:29.410
I'm looking at, to look at like
for like. I'm looking at all countries

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:35.590
that gained colonial independence in the
1940s and 50s and 60s and looking at

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:39.970
their founding constitutions and
creating an index of inclusivity based

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:44.049
on such questions as: does the preamble of
the Constitution, the part that begins

00:03:44.049 --> 00:03:51.220
with "We the People," is there a definition
of the nation that mentions race,

00:03:51.220 --> 00:04:02.079
religion or region. Is there a religious
or racial category that's required for

00:04:02.079 --> 00:04:06.819
to either be at the head of state or to
serve in an important distinction of

00:04:06.819 --> 00:04:10.630
government. And using these kinds of
questions I create an inclusivity

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:17.950
index that I'm statistically looking at
whether it has that, has a pattern with a

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:22.120
whole bunch of databases that look at
democracy. So I'm in the process right

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:25.810
now of putting that together. So that's
the first way I do that.

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:29.590
The second way that I do that
(investigate the relationship between

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:37.120
nationalism and democracy) is that I'm
looking at some experiments which show

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:42.730
that when you look at, you ask
individuals how much are you willing to

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:48.910
give to co-ethnics or
co-religionists in need. And that when

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:53.680
you actually prime individuals, for
example, by showing them a national flag

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:58.030
they're actually,
everyone is more likely to give to their

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:02.860
co-ethnics in most contexts. But when you
prime individuals by showing the

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:09.070
national flag, they actually are less
likely to want to give to -

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:13.930
they're equally likely to want to give
to members of their nation. So it very

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:18.669
much shows that nationalism is a force
for solidarity. And the third way, and

00:05:18.669 --> 00:05:22.450
that's the one I'm going to talk about
a little bit more tonight, is that I'm

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:28.300
looking at countries that are largely
similar, so three sets of countries that

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:32.800
are largely similar on those
explanations I gave you, but that have

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.630
very different national identities at
their founding. And I trace over time

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:51.209
whether and how those national
identities get used in moments of crisis.

